{
  "summary": "Adds wallet and RPC support for sending transactions to BIP 352 Silent Payment addresses via send, sendtoaddress, sendmany, and sendall. It introduces PaymentDestination to abstract both static script destinations and dynamic BIP 352 destinations whose taproot scriptPubKeys derive from spending input private keys. Coin selection is updated to ensure shared-secret-eligible inputs are included, and recipient destination metadata is persisted in the wallet database so transactions can be RBF fee-bumped.",
  "problem": "Bitcoin Core cannot send funds to BIP 352 silent payment addresses. Because silent payments derive unique taproot outputs dynamically from the private keys of the inputs being spent, Core's existing destination model, coin selection, transaction building, and fee-bumping mechanisms cannot handle them.",
  "evidence": [
    "Part of the BIP 352 silent payments roadmap tracked under project issue #28536 and second iteration of #28201.",
    "PR description highlights essential safety constraints: 'any operation that produces a silent payments tx that is not safe to fee bump later is blocked; this includes operations that create psbts and send with add_to_wallet=False'."
  ],
  "dependencies": {
    "depends_on": [
      "#35301"
    ],
    "enables": [
      "#32966"
    ]
  },
  "scope_notes": "Touches wallet transaction creation, coin selection, database persistence, and RPC handling across 43 files. More than half of the diff is test code, including an independent BIP 352 scanning implementation in the functional test framework.",
  "changed_since_previous": "",
  "card": "Adds wallet and RPC support for sending transactions to BIP 352 Silent Payment addresses via send, sendtoaddress, sendmany, and sendall. Bitcoin Core wallet users currently cannot send to silent payment addresses, preventing the use of reusable privacy-preserving addresses without address reuse. The PR introduces PaymentDestination to handle dynamic taproot script derivation from input private keys, updates coin selection to ensure derivation-eligible keys are selected, and persists recipient metadata to support RBF fee-bumping. Operations that cannot safely fee-bump or derive outputs (such as external signers, PSBT generation, and unadded transactions) are explicitly blocked. This work is the second iteration of #28201, depends on prerequisite PR #35301, and progresses the BIP 352 roadmap tracked in #28536.",
